Here is the bug that quietly ships to production. The username arrives at runtime — from a form, a file, or a database — so it is a brand-new object, not a pooled literal. Comparing it with `==` asks the wrong question.

```java
// Wrong: == compares references, not text
String stored = new String("admin"); // a fresh object, not from the pool
if (stored == "admin") {             // false — different objects
    System.out.println("welcome");
}
// Prints nothing, even though the text matches.
```

The characters are identical, yet the login silently fails. What makes this dangerous is that a unit test written with two literals would pass — the bug only appears once real data flows in.

The fix is to compare content with `.equals()`, and to put the known literal on the left so a `null` username can never blow up.

```java
// Right: .equals() compares characters; literal first avoids NPE
String stored = new String("admin");
if ("admin".equals(stored)) {  // true — same characters
    System.out.println("welcome");
}
```

Putting the literal first means a `null` value in `stored` returns `false` instead of throwing `NullPointerException` — `"admin".equals(null)` is simply `false`, while `stored.equals("admin")` would crash if `stored` were null.

Order is everything with `&&`. Here the method call sits on the left and the null check on the right, so Java reaches the method *before* it has confirmed the object is non-null.

```java
String user = null;
// Wrong order: user.length() runs before the null check
if (user.length() > 0 && user != null) { // NullPointerException on user.length()
    System.out.println("ok");
}
```

The `user != null` on the right is useless here — `user.length()` already threw a `NullPointerException` before evaluation ever got that far. A guard only protects what comes *after* it.

Put the null check on the left and `&&` turns it into a gate: if `user` is null, the left side is `false`, the expression is already decided, and the method call is never reached.

```java
String user = null;
// Right order: if user is null, length() is never reached
if (user != null && user.length() > 0) { // safe — prints nothing
    System.out.println("ok");
}
```

Because `&&` evaluates left-to-right and stops on `false`, the null check must come first — it acts as a gate protecting the method call on the right. Flip the two and you are back to the crash above.

Watch what it takes to assign one of two values with `if-else`: you have to declare the variable empty first, then fill it inside each branch.

```java
int score = 85;
String grade;
if (score >= 90) {
    grade = "A";
} else {
    grade = "B";
}
// grade can't even be final here
```

Four lines of logic for a single binary choice, and `grade` cannot be `final` because it is declared before it is assigned. The `if-else` is a statement — it executes blocks, it does not produce a value you can assign in one go.

Because the ternary is an expression, the whole thing collapses to a single value you assign directly.

```java
int score = 85;
String grade = score >= 90 ? "A" : "B"; // same logic, one line
// grade can even be final now
final String display = score > 70 ? "Pass" : "Fail";
System.out.println(display); // Pass
```

Because the ternary is an expression, `grade` can be declared `final` — something impossible with an `if-else` block that needs to assign after declaration. That "produces a value" property is the crisp distinction the interviewer is listening for.
